      "A finite verb changes to show tense and agrees with its subject: she swims (present tense, third person singular), she swam (past tense). A non-finite verb form does neither — on its own it shows no tense and never agrees with a subject. English builds three non-finite forms from a base verb: the gerund (swimming), which functions as a noun; the infinitive (to swim, or the bare base swim after certain verbs), which fills verb-like slots such as the object of another verb; and the participle (swimming or swum), which functions as an adjective or opens a participle clause. All three can come from the very same base verb, yet they play completely different grammatical roles — knowing which is which is the key to using any of them correctly.",
